devsys: build NitrOS9 Level 2 programs on the F256 itself

The tools (in /dd/CMDS; "help NAME" describes each):
  las     the assembler: lwasm's syntax (NitrOS9's sources) and RMA's
  llink   the linker: ROF objects and libraries into a module
  dcc     the C compiler (runs dcpp, dcc68, dco68, las and llink)
  make    rebuilds what changed (rules in /dd/SYS/make.default)

Where things are:
  /dd/DEFS            C headers (stdio.h ...)
  /dd/DEFS/NITROS9    NitrOS9 main's assembler defs: defsfile, os9.d,
                      wildbits.d ...
  /dd/LIB             C libraries (clib.l, sys.l, cstart.r) and
                      NitrOS9 main's alib.l, net.l, wildbitsl2.l
  /dd/SYS/n9mod.opt   las options for a NitrOS9 module source
  /dd/SYS/n9obj.opt   las options for a NitrOS9 object source (section)
  /dd/DEVSYS/EX       the examples below

A NitrOS9 module ("mod", "use defsfile"):
  chd /dd/devsys/ex
  las hello.asm --args=/dd/sys/n9mod.opt -o=hello
  attr hello e pe
  /dd/devsys/ex/hello      (a bare name runs from /dd/CMDS)

An object program linked with NitrOS9's libraries (grep, from main):
  las grep.as --args=/dd/sys/n9obj.opt -o=grep.o
  llink grep.o -l=/dd/lib/wildbitsl2.l -l=/dd/lib/net.l -l=/dd/lib/alib.l -o=/dd/devsys/ex/grep
  /dd/devsys/ex/grep -n "las" /dd/devsys/README

A C program:
  dcc hello.c -f=/dd/devsys/ex/hi
  /dd/devsys/ex/hi
  dcc hello.c -w             (only checks for errors)

make (C and assembly, see EX/M/makefile):
  chd /dd/devsys/ex/m
  make
  mkdemo

Good to know:
- An options file holds NitrOS9 main's -D settings and pragmas, the
  defs directory and a defs image. Add your own options after it.
- The defs image (/dd/SYS/n9mod.img, n9obj.img) is recorded by the
  first assembly that uses it and makes the next ones about twice as
  fast. It is used when nothing is defined before "use defsfile" and
  the options are the same; otherwise las reads the defs as usual.
  After a defs file changes, the next assembly records it again.
- las writes its output to the current directory; llink and dcc -f=
  write a bare name to the execution directory (/dd/CMDS), so give
  them a full path to put the program somewhere else.
- OS-9 will not redirect into a file that exists: delete it first.
- NitrOS9's grep (and one built from grep.as) ends with an error
  number such as ERROR #115 even when it worked: a bug in main's
  alib (FGETS_NOCR loses the end-of-file code), not in the build.
- /dd/DEVSYS/selftest checks the whole disk (results in
  /dd/DEVSYS/R). It runs once per card write.
